<g class="node store" data-k="data" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="HuBMAP kidney data"><path class="shape" d="M110,21 A110.0,7 0 0 1 330,21 V63 A110.0,7 0 0 1 110,63 Z"/><path class="rim" d="M110,21 A110.0,7 0 0 0 330,21" fill="none"/><text class="nt" x="220.0" y="43.0" text-anchor="middle">HuBMAP kidney data</text><text class="ns" x="220.0" y="57.0" text-anchor="middle">tiles + vessel polygons</text></g>
<g class="node box" data-k="split" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Slide-level split"><rect class="shape" x="110" y="118" width="220" height="44" rx="5"/><text class="nt" x="220.0" y="138.0" text-anchor="middle">Slide-level split</text><text class="ns" x="220.0" y="152.0" text-anchor="middle">train · val · test</text></g>
<g class="node arm" data-k="a_base" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Baseline"><rect class="shape" x="14.5" y="222" width="96" height="40" rx="20.0" style="stroke:var(--c-base)"/><text class="nt" x="62.5" y="246.0" text-anchor="middle">Baseline</text></g>
<g class="node arm" data-k="a_hed" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="HED"><rect class="shape" x="119.5" y="222" width="96" height="40" rx="20.0" style="stroke:var(--c-hed)"/><text class="nt" x="167.5" y="246.0" text-anchor="middle">HED</text></g>
<g class="node arm" data-k="a_mac" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Macenko"><rect class="shape" x="224.5" y="222" width="96" height="40" rx="20.0" style="stroke:var(--c-mac)"/><text class="nt" x="272.5" y="246.0" text-anchor="middle">Macenko</text></g>
<g class="node arm" data-k="a_full" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="HED + Mac."><rect class="shape" x="329.5" y="222" width="96" height="40" rx="20.0" style="stroke:var(--c-full)"/><text class="nt" x="377.5" y="246.0" text-anchor="middle">HED + Mac.</text></g>
<g class="node box" data-k="train" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Train and evaluate"><rect class="shape" x="70" y="316" width="300" height="48" rx="5"/><text class="nt" x="220.0" y="338.0" text-anchor="middle">Train and evaluate</text><text class="ns" x="220.0" y="352.0" text-anchor="middle">U-Net · per-tile Dice</text></g>
<g class="node store" data-k="sum" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Results summary"><path class="shape" d="M120,423 A100.0,7 0 0 1 320,423 V465 A100.0,7 0 0 1 120,465 Z"/><path class="rim" d="M120,423 A100.0,7 0 0 0 320,423" fill="none"/><text class="nt" x="220.0" y="445.0" text-anchor="middle">Results summary</text><text class="ns" x="220.0" y="459.0" text-anchor="middle">one JSON per sweep</text></g>
</svg>
      <figcaption class="cap after"><b>Figure {{ sec.fig_pipeline|label }}.</b> Data flow from raw annotations to results. Runs differ only at the four arms. Select a stage for details.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <aside class="pipe-panel" id="pipe-panel" aria-live="polite"></aside>
  </section>

  {# Pipeline panel content, one <template> per node. site.js clones the
     selected one into #pipe-panel. Kept as HTML so the number check sees it. #}
  <template id="node-data">
    <p class="pp-k">Data</p>
    <h3>HuBMAP kidney data</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ paths.raw|label }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">{{ cfg.image_size|n }} × {{ cfg.image_size|n }} tiles of PAS-stained human kidney with {{ n_vessels|n }} blood-vessel outlines across {{ n_annotated|n }} annotated tiles. Polygons are rasterised into binary masks; glomeruli and uncertain regions are discarded. Vessels cover {{ coverage|pct }} of a tile on average, which drives the loss choice downstream.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-split">
    <p class="pp-k">Preprocessing</p>
    <h3>Slide-level split</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ paths.splits|label }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">Tiles are grouped by whole-slide image, because tiles from one slide share stain, scanner and patient. Train: {{ splits.train|n }} tiles from one expert-labelled slide. Validation: {{ splits.val|n }} tiles from another. Test: {{ splits.test|n }} tiles from two held-out slides with model-generated labels. Any overlap between splits aborts the run.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-a_base">
    <p class="pp-k">Arm</p>
    <h3>Baseline</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ arm_id.base }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">Flips, {{ rot_deg|n }}° rotations and brightness/contrast jitter, with no stain handling. The control every other arm is measured against.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-a_hed">
    <p class="pp-k">Arm</p>
    <h3>HED augmentation</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ arm_id.hed }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">Adds multiplicative and additive jitter to each stain channel in optical-density space (σ = {{ hed.sigma|num }}, applied to {{ hed.p|pct(0) }} of training tiles). Tests whether exposure to stain variation improves transfer to unseen slides.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-a_mac">
    <p class="pp-k">Arm</p>
    <h3>Macenko normalisation</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ arm_id.mac }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">Re-expresses every tile against a fixed reference stain matrix, at training and at evaluation. Tests whether removing stain variation helps. The reference is fitted on PAS: the per-entry median stain matrix over the {{ splits.train|n }} training-slide tiles, so validation and test never inform it. Normalised evaluation tiles are cached, so the per-tile SVD runs once rather than every epoch.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-a_full">
    <p class="pp-k">Arm</p>
    <h3>HED + Macenko</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ arm_id.full }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">Macenko normalisation then HED jitter during training, Macenko normalisation at evaluation, so training and evaluation inputs share the normalised distribution. Tests whether the two effects add, as reported on H&amp;E by Tellez et al. {{ cite("tellez") }}.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-train">
    <p class="pp-k">Training</p>
    <h3>Train and evaluate</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ paths.train|label }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">U-Net with an ImageNet-pretrained ResNet-34 encoder on full-resolution tiles. Equal-weight soft Dice + BCE, AdamW (lr {{ cfg.lr|pow10 }}), cosine schedule, at most {{ cfg.epochs|n }} epochs with early stopping at patience {{ cfg.early_stop_patience|n }}. The best validation checkpoint is scored on every validation and test tile. Repeated for every seed; arms sharing a seed share initialisation, which makes the comparison paired.</p>
  </template>
  <template id="node-sum">
    <p class="pp-k">Data</p>
    <h3>Results summary</h3>
    <p class="pp-f"><code>{{ paths.summary|label }}</code></p>
    <p class="pp-d">One entry per run: configuration, learning curves and per-tile Dice and IoU. Written after every run, so an interrupted sweep resumes where it stopped. Every number on this page is computed from it.</p>
  </template>

  <section class="col">
    <h2><span class="n">{{ sec.design|label }}</span>Design decisions</h2>

    <h3>{{ sec.split|label }} Slide-level splitting</h3>
    <p>Tiles from the same whole-slide image share stain, scanner and patient, so a tile-level split would let the model score well by recognising the slide. Training and validation each use one expert-annotated slide; the test set is two held-out slides from the second dataset partition, whose labels are model-generated and therefore noisier. The split builder checks the configured test slides against the held-out pool and refuses to run on any overlap, so leakage is a hard failure rather than a silent one.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="col">
    <h3>{{ sec.stain|label }} Stain transforms in optical-density space</h3>
    <p>Both stain transforms were implemented from first principles rather than taken from a library, so that their behaviour could be inspected directly and the same stain operation reused in the demo's stress test. RGB is converted to optical density, where stain contributions are additive, and projected onto stain axes with the Ruifrok–Johnston matrix {{ cite("ruifrok") }}. HED augmentation then perturbs each stain channel with a multiplicative and an additive term drawn from <span class="num">U(−σ, σ)</span>, with <span class="num">σ = {{ hed.sigma|num }}</span>. Working in this space means the perturbation mimics a change in stain uptake rather than an arbitrary colour shift.</p>
    <p>Macenko normalisation {{ cite("macenko") }} estimates each tile's two stain vectors from the plane of the top two singular vectors of its foreground optical densities (each sign-fixed, so the estimate does not depend on pixel order), takes the robust angular extremes in that plane, and re-expresses the tile's concentrations against a fixed reference stain matrix, fitted on PAS as the per-entry median over the {{ splits.train|n }} training-slide tiles. Tiles with too little tissue to estimate stains are passed through unchanged rather than halting a run.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="col">
    <h3>{{ sec.arms|label }} Experimental arms</h3>
    <p>The arms differ only in their colour transforms. Geometric augmentation (flips and {{ rot_deg|n }}° rotations), architecture, optimiser and data are shared. Normalisation is applied at evaluation as well as training where the arm requires it, which is what distinguishes the combined arm from the augmentation-only arm.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="wide scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th class="l">Training colour transform</th><th class="l">Evaluation colour transform</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>{{ sw("base") }}Baseline <code>{{ arm_id.base }}</code></td><td class="l">brightness / contrast</td><td class="l">none</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{{ sw("hed") }}HED <code>{{ arm_id.hed }}</code></td><td class="l">HED jitter, <i>p</i> = {{ hed.p|num }}</td><td class="l">none</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{{ sw("mac") }}Macenko <code>{{ arm_id.mac }}</code></td><td class="l">Macenko</td><td class="l">Macenko</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{{ sw("full") }}HED + Macenko <code>{{ arm_id.full }}</code></td><td class="l">Macenko, then HED jitter, <i>p</i> = {{ hed.p|num }}</td><td class="l">Macenko</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <section class="col">
    <h3>{{ sec.training|label }} Training</h3>
    <p>A U-Net with an ImageNet-pretrained ResNet-34 encoder (segmentation-models-pytorch) is trained on full-resolution {{ cfg.image_size|n }} × {{ cfg.image_size|n }} tiles, since downsampling erases the thinnest vessels. Vessels occupy about {{ coverage|pct }} of pixels, so the loss is an equal-weight sum of soft Dice and BCE: the Dice term targets the evaluation metric under heavy class imbalance, and the BCE term stabilises early gradients. Optimisation uses AdamW (lr {{ cfg.lr|pow10 }}, weight decay {{ cfg.weight_decay|pow10 }}) with cosine annealing over at most {{ cfg.epochs|n }} epochs, early stopping at a patience of {{ cfg.early_stop_patience|n }}, and restoration of the best validation state.</p>
    <p>Normalised evaluation tiles are cached in memory, so the per-tile SVD runs once rather than every epoch.</p>

    <h3>{{ sec.repro|label }} Reproducibility</h3>
    <p>Each run is seeded end to end, with cuDNN autotuning disabled and deterministic GPU kernels enforced, so a run's outcome is a function of its seed rather than of scheduling noise. The sweep writes its results after every run and skips completed ones on restart, and every number on this page is read from a single committed summary file.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="col">
    <h3>{{ sec.stats|label }} Statistical analysis</h3>
    <p>Each arm was trained under {{ n_seeds|n }} seeds, and runs sharing a seed share their initialisation, so comparisons with the baseline are paired at the seed level. The unit of analysis is a run's mean per-tile Dice. Effects are reported as mean paired differences with percentile-bootstrap {{ ci_level|pct(0) }} CIs ({{ n_boot|n }} resamples), tested with a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test and Holm-adjusted across the three comparisons. A paired design was chosen because seed-to-seed variation in the baseline (s.d. {{ T.arms.base.std|f3 }}) is as large as the effect of interest, so unpaired comparisons of single runs could easily point the wrong way. Additivity is assessed with the interaction contrast <span class="num">Δ<sub>HED+Mac</sub> − Δ<sub>HED</sub> − Δ<sub>Mac</sub></span>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="col">
    <h2><span class="n">{{ sec.results|label }}</span>Results</h2>
  </section>

  <figure class="wide">
    <p class="cap"><b>Table {{ sec.table_results|label }}.</b> Dice by arm, mean ± s.d. across seeds. Δ: mean paired difference from the baseline with bootstrap {{ ci_level|pct(0) }} CI. <i>p</i>: two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank, Holm-adjusted. Test: {{ T.n_tiles|n }} tiles from two held-out slides with model-generated labels. Validation: {{ V.n_tiles|n }} tiles from one expert-annotated slide, also used for checkpoint selection.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table class="res">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Arm</th>
            <th>Test Dice</th>
            <th>Δ test <span class="sub">[{{ ci_level|pct(0) }} CI]</span></th>
            <th>Seeds improved</th>
            <th><i>p</i><sub>Holm</sub></th>
            <th>Val. Dice</th>
            <th>Δ val. <span class="sub">[{{ ci_level|pct(0) }} CI]</span></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>{{ sw("base") }}Baseline</td>
            <td{{ bold("test_dice", "base") }}>{{ sd(T.arms.base) }}</td><td>n/a</td><td>n/a</td><td>n/a</td>
            <td{{ bold("val_dice", "base") }}>{{ sd(V.arms.base) }}</td><td>n/a</td>
          </tr>
          {% for k, name in [("hed", "HED"), ("mac", "Macenko"), ("full", "HED + Macenko")] %}
          <tr>
            <td>{{ sw(k) }}{{ name }}</td>
            <td{{ bold("test_dice", k) }}>{{ sd(T.arms[k]) }}</td><td{{ bold("test_delta", k) }}>{{ dci(T.vs[k]) }}</td><td>{{ T.vs[k].improved|n }}/{{ T.vs[k].n|n }}</td><td>{{ T.vs[k].p_holm|pval }}</td>
            <td{{ bold("val_dice", k) }}>{{ sd(V.arms[k]) }}</td><td{{ bold("val_delta", k) }}>{{ dci(V.vs[k]) }}</td>
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <section class="col">
    <p><b>HED augmentation.</b> The improvement was consistent across seeds ({{ T.vs.hed.improved|n }} of {{ T.vs.hed.n|n }}, <span class="num">d<sub>z</sub> = {{ T.vs.hed.dz|f2 }}</span>) and replicated on the validation slide (<span class="num">{{ dci(V.vs.hed) }}</span>, <span class="num"><i>p</i><sub>Holm</sub> = {{ V.vs.hed.p_holm|pval }}</span>). The seed-to-seed spread on validation also halved (s.d. <span class="num">{{ V.arms.hed.std|f3 }}</span> vs <span class="num">{{ V.arms.base.std|f3 }}</span>), consistent with stain augmentation stabilising training as well as shifting its mean.</p>
    <p><b>Macenko normalisation.</b> Normalisation had no detectable effect on test Dice (<span class="num">{{ dci(T.vs.mac) }}</span>, {{ T.vs.mac.improved|n }} of {{ T.vs.mac.n|n }} seeds, <span class="num"><i>p</i><sub>Holm</sub> = {{ T.vs.mac.p_holm|pval }}</span>) and improved validation Dice (<span class="num">{{ dci(V.vs.mac) }}</span>, <span class="num"><i>p</i><sub>Holm</sub> = {{ V.vs.mac.p_holm|pval }}</span>).</p>
    <p><b>Additivity.</b> The interaction contrast was <span class="num">{{ dci(T.interaction) }}</span> on test and <span class="num">{{ dci(V.interaction) }}</span> on validation. Both intervals include zero, so there is no evidence that the two effects fail to add, consistent with the result reported on H&amp;E {{ cite("tellez") }}. The combined arm, trained and evaluated on normalised inputs, was the strongest arm on validation (<span class="num">{{ dci(V.vs.full) }}</span>, <span class="num"><i>p</i><sub>Holm</sub> = {{ V.vs.full.p_holm|pval }}</span>); its test gain (<span class="num">{{ dci(T.vs.full) }}</span>) did not survive correction (<span class="num"><i>p</i><sub>Holm</sub> = {{ T.vs.full.p_holm|pval }}</span>).</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="wide">
    <div class="panels">
      <div class="panel"><h4>(a) Test <span>· {{ T.n_tiles|n }} tiles, {{ slides(splits.test_wsis) }}</span></h4><svg id="pa" data-split="test" role="img" aria-label="Paired differences in test Dice from the baseline for each arm"></svg></div>
      <div class="panel"><h4>(b) Validation <span>· {{ V.n_tiles|n }} tiles, {{ slides(splits.val_wsis) }}</span></h4><svg id="pb" data-split="val" role="img" aria-label="Paired differences in validation Dice from the baseline for each arm"></svg></div>
    </div>
    <p class="cap after"><b>Figure {{ sec.fig_results|label }}.</b> Paired difference in mean Dice from the baseline. Each point is one seed; bars show the mean and bootstrap {{ ci_level|pct(0) }} CI. Points right of the dashed line are seeds on which the arm outperformed the baseline trained from the same initialisation.</p>
  </figure>

  <section class="col">
    <h2><span class="n">{{ sec.limits|label }}</span>Limitations and next steps</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>Training uses a single slide. Stain diversity in training is the quantity under study, so this is the main constraint on generalisation.</li>
      <li>Test labels are model-generated. Dice on test supports comparisons between arms, not estimates of absolute accuracy.</li>
      <li>Validation Dice also selects the checkpoint, so validation estimates are optimistically biased.</li>
      <li>The PAS reference is fitted on the single training slide, so it inherits that slide's stain.</li>
      <li>Next: train on more than one slide, and fit the reference stain matrix across slides.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>References</h2>
    <ol class="refs">
      {% for ref in refs %}
      <li id="ref-{{ ref.no }}" tabindex="-1">{{ ref.html }}</li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ol>
  </section>
</article>
</div>

<script type="application/json" id="fig-data">{{ fig|tojson }}</script>
<script src="site.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
